<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mon people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mon are a people of Lower Myanmar and central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> who speak a Mon-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> (Austroasiatic) language. They were among the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist peoples of mainland South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, associated with the Dvaravati culture and with kingdoms such as Thaton, Hariphunchai and Hanthawaddy. Their script and religious learning shaped Burmese and northern Thai culture, and many Mon fled to Siam after Burmese conquests in the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mon-people.jpg" alt="Mon people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninjastrikers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mon_State_(2018).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mon descend from Proto-Austroasiatic people, who are believed to have migrated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> valley in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> between 3,000 and 2,000 BCE along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a>, Sittaung, Irrawaddy and Ping rivers, bringing riverine agriculture and wet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Their language belongs to the Monic branch of the Austroasiatic family and is close to Nyah Kur, spoken in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p>The Mon were among the earliest peoples of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and founded some of its earliest civilisations: Dvaravati in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, Sri Gotapura in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, the Hariphunchai Kingdom in northern Thailand and the Thaton Kingdom in Lower Myanmar. They were the first to receive Theravada Buddhist missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and they adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a>; the oldest Mon inscription, found in a cave at Saraburi, dates to about 550 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Pagan Kingdom collapsed in 1287, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wareru">Wareru</a>, born to a Mon mother and a Tai father, made himself king of the Mon at Martaban and later moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>. His Hanthawaddy kingdom lasted from 1287 to 1539. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnya-u">Binnya U</a> defended it against Lan Na in the mid-14th century, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razadarit">Razadarit</a>, who reigned from 1384 to 1421, repelled invasions from Ava and made contributions to the Shwedagon Pagoda.</p><p>In 1539 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> of Taungoo took Hanthawaddy after a siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> lasting four years. Many Mon refugees fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, where the Mon aristocracy joined the court. The fall of Martaban in 1541 was accompanied by massacre and pillage on a large scale. In 1740 Mon rebels, joined by the Gwe Shan, restored a Hanthawaddy kingdom with French support, but King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a> conquered it in 1757. Tens of thousands of Mon civilians were killed, among them over 3,000 monks in the capital alone.</p><p>Under the Konbaung dynasty further rebellions, including one in 1814, were put down harshly, and large numbers of Mon migrated to Siam. The British took control of the Mon territories after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> in 1852, but never restored the Mon kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Today the Mon are a major ethnic group in Myanmar and a minor one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, and the two Mon dialects are mutually intelligible. The Tai Tham and Burmese alphabets are adaptations of the Mon script, and Burmese has borrowed vocabulary from Mon, especially for administration, architecture, cloth, cuisine and flowers. Cities including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a> were founded by Mon people or Mon rulers. Their symbol is the hongsa, a mythological water bird. Mon National Day, set up in 1947, commemorates the founding of Hanthawaddy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mon people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1740 the <b>Mon</b> of Lower Burma rebelled, and the forces of the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom, after a decade of fighting, advanced up the Irrawaddy and took Ava in 1752.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The war ended the <b>Mon people</b>'s centuries-long dominance of the south.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Pagan Kingdom, Wareru, a <b>Mon</b> adventurer said to have been a merchant, seized Martaban in 1287 and gained recognition from Sukhothai.</blockquote><a href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>She brought <b>Mon</b> Buddhist monks and craftsmen from the Dvaravati world, and one of her twin sons founded Lampang as a second centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/hariphunchai">Hariphunchai</a></li><li><blockquote>Lavo emerged as one of the <b>Mon</b> towns of the Dvaravati world, with Lopburi as its centre from about the 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/lavo-kingdom">Lavo Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>He built a fort and church, encouraged Catholic conversions and allied with local <b>Mon</b> leaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-syriam">Portuguese Syriam</a></li></ul></section>
